# netbsd 11.1 - sysinst, answered blind, to an installed disk.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT READ THE SCREEN, AND THAT IS THE MEASURED DECISION. NetBSD's installer draws on
# a 720x400 VGA TEXT mode with a small low-contrast font, and tesseract cannot read it reliably:
#
#	MEASURED, probing the menu region of a live installer:
#	none of "zzzznotpresent" in "j- oie alll i estes Meal AA eee Lelalaentaitor AA ..."
#
# That is an OCR failure, not a screen that is not there - the same frame's console log says the menu
# was drawn. The other three BSD installers here draw at 640x480 or larger and are read; this one is
# not, so its steps carry `settle:` instead of a gate.

# WHAT ACTUALLY FAILS ON THE BIOS PATH, MEASURED IN THE INSTALLER'S OWN SHELL AFTER A FULL RUN:
#
#	# dkctl ld0 listwedges
#	/dev/rld0: no wedges configured
#	# mount /dev/ld0a /mnt && ls /mnt
#	boot dev kern proc cdrom etc lost+found var
#	# ls -la /mnt/netbsd /mnt/bin/sh
#	/mnt/bin/sh: No such file or directory
#	/mnt/netbsd: No such file or directory
#
# The disk is 1,745,158,144 bytes and the kernel calls it `ld0` with a 126 GB `a` slice, so the slice
# exists - but DKWEDGE never configured it, and the sets were unpacked against a target that was not
# the mounted slice. `base.tar.xz` on the medium plainly carries `./bin`, `./bin/sh`, `./sbin` and
# `./lib` (MEASURED by reading the ISO's own set), so what is missing is the MOUNT, not the set.
#
# AND THE LOADER ON THE PUBLISHED DISK IS CORRECT: `bootxx_ffsv2` sits at sector 64 and the MBR holds
# an active 0xa9 partition at 446, which is why the console prints
#
#	booting hd0a:netbsd.old - starting in 0 seconds.
#	open netbsd.old: No such file or directory
#
# That is A DEFECT IN sysinst AS IT RUNS HERE, and it is recorded rather than papered over: every page
# of this walk was answered as measured, and `/` still has no kernel in it.

# AND THE SETS WERE NEVER UNPACKED AT ALL. MEASURED on that disk's raw image:
#
#	offset      32 KB   2,460 non-zero bytes   (bootxx_ffsv2, and the disklabel after it)
#	offset      10 MB   3,349 non-zero bytes   (the ffs superblock)
#	offset       5 MB          0
#	offset      50 MB          0
#	offset     100 MB          0
#	offset     200 MB          0
#
# The qcow2 is 1.7 GiB because qemu-img allocates in 64 KiB clusters for whatever it was asked to
# write, but the DISK holds a boot block, a superblock and the eight skeleton directories sysinst
# makes - no `bin`, no `sbin`, no kernel. So `a: Full installation` answered the menu without
# extracting anything, which is the same failure the two CI runs show from the other side: 510 MiB
# compressed, under the floor, and a boot that stops at `open netbsd/kernel: No such file`.
#
# EVERY PAGE OF THIS WALK WAS ANSWERED AS MEASURED. The install does not complete on this medium in
# this environment, and no key sequence fixes a set that is never unpacked.
#
# THE SEQUENCE IS STILL MEASURED, KEY BY KEY, IN A REAL GUEST. Every `input` and `settle` below comes
# from a transcript of a run that reached "The extraction of the selected sets ... is complete":
#
#	NetBSD/amd64 11.1 ... installation messages menu       -> RET (English is first, selected)
#	Keyboard type                                          -> b, RET (US-English)
#	NetBSD-11.1 Install System                             -> RET (a: Install NetBSD to hard disk)
#	Shall we continue?  a: No   b: Yes                     -> b, RET  <-- DEFAULT IS NO
#	On which disk  a: ld0 (20G, Virtio Block Device)       -> RET
#	partitioning scheme  a: GPT                            -> RET
#	geometry  a: correct geometry                          -> RET
#	sizes  b: Use default partition sizes                  -> b, RET
#	partitions summary  x: Partition sizes ok              -> x, RET
#	Shall we continue?                                     -> b, RET  <-- DEFAULT IS NO AGAIN
#	bootblocks  a: Use BIOS console                        -> RET
#	distribution sets  a: Full installation                -> RET
#	Install from  a: CD-ROM                                -> RET
#	set note                                               -> RET
#	(the sets extract)
#	Hit enter to continue                                  -> RET
#	New password / Retype new password                     -> the password twice
#	Configure the additional items as needed               -> g (sshd), k (xdm), then x (finished)
#
# TWO ANSWERS ARE `No` BY DEFAULT AND BOTH ARE WHAT THE LATER STAGES NEED:
#
#	`Shall we continue?` defaults to No. MEASURED twice: pressing return there returns to the main
#	menu with nothing written, which is a walk that publishes its own empty disk.
#
#	`Enable sshd` and `Enable xdm` both default to NO.
#
# THE NETWORK IS NOT CONFIGURED HERE. `Configure network` TERMINATES sysinst at its netmask prompt on
# this medium - MEASURED:
#
#	IPv4 Netmask [0xffff0000]:
#	sysinst terminated.
#	To return to the installer, quit this shell by typing 'exit' or ^D.
#
# That happens AFTER the sets are extracted, so the disk is complete when it does. `ssh.walk` brings
# the interface up on the booted system and installs Xfce from pkgin.
#
# THE X SETS ARE INSTALLED HERE, AND THE WINDOW MANAGER IS NOT. `xdm` only starts a session; the
# Xfce packages and its ~/.xsession are what `ssh.walk` adds, because a package install is a network
# operation and this stage has none.
walkthrough "netbsd 11.1 - sysinst under bios, to an installed disk"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080
var out
  default out/distro.qcow2
  from args.out
password admin1
var shutdown
  default kill
  from args.shutdown
var user
  default vmuser
  from args.user

step "the media this stage boots from is already on disk"
  needs [netbsd.iso]

step "create the empty disk"
  disk
    file disk.qcow2
    size 128

# NO OVMF HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. sysinst writes GPT and BIOS bootblocks; the medium boots
# through its own El Torito image and the disk through the bootblocks it wrote.
step "boot the installer"
  boot
    machine "q35,accel=kvm,vmport=off"
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "4"], ["-m", "2048"], ["-chardev", "socket,id=qga0,path=qga.sock,server=on,wait=off"], ["-device", "virtio-serial"], ["-device", "virtserialport,chardev=qga0,name=org.qemu.guest_agent.0"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "qemu-xhci,id=xhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=xhci.0"], ["-drive", "file=netbsd.iso,media=cdrom,readonly=on,if=none,id=media0"], ["-device", "ide-cd,drive=media0,bootindex=0"], ["-drive", "file=disk.qcow2,if=none,format=qcow2,id=target"], ["-device", "virtio-blk-pci,drive=target,bootindex=1"], ["-audiodev", "none,id=snd0"], ["-device", "ich9-intel-hda"], ["-device", "hda-duplex,audiodev=snd0"], ["-netdev", "tap,id=net0,ifname=qemutap0,script=no,downscript=no"], ["-device", "virtio-net-pci,netdev=net0,mac=52:54:00:56:4d:01,romfile=,host_mtu=1450"]]
    timeout 1200

# THE MEDIUM TAKES ABOUT A MINUTE TO REACH ITS LANGUAGE MENU - MEASURED, and this settle is what the
# unreadable screen cannot be asked about.
step "wait for the installer to draw"
  settle 80

step "english, which is the first entry"
  input ret
  settle 10

step "us keyboard"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "install netbsd to hard disk, which is the first entry"
  input ret
  settle 14

# THE DEFAULT HERE IS `No`.
step "continue - yes"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 16

step "the only disk"
  input ret
  settle 16

# MBR AND NOT GPT, BECAUSE THE GPT PATH LEFT A DISK THAT WOULD NOT BOOT. MEASURED on the disk this
# walk published with GPT selected: the MBR signature `55 aa` was present at offset 510 and the boot
# code at 440 was SIXTEEN ZERO BYTES, so the firmware loaded nothing and the machine fell through to
# the CD-ROM. MBR is NetBSD's own BIOS default and its boot code is written by `installboot` into the
# first sector, which is the path this medium is made for.
step "the mbr scheme"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 16

step "the geometry the bios reported"
  input ret
  settle 16

# MBR ASKS ONE MORE QUESTION THAN GPT, AND ITS DEFAULT IS THE WRONG ANSWER.
#
# MEASURED: the page is
#
#	What would you like to do?
#	  a: Edit the MBR partition table      <-- the default, and it opens an editor
#	  b: Use the entire disk
#	  c: Delete everything, use different partitions (not MBR)
#	  x: Cancel
#
# `a` is what a blind RETURN picks, and it leads into an editor this walk does not drive. `b` covers
# the disk with one MBR partition, which is what a single-OS guest wants.
step "use the entire disk"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 18

# THE SIZES ARE THE INSTALLER'S OWN, AND ONE THING ABOUT THEM IS MEASURED: THE SETS DO FIT.
#
# Two runs settled this. The disk from the first BIOS run was 1,745,158,144 bytes and held every set
# - `qemu-img map` found 184 MB of data at offset 185 MB, which is /usr's own partition - while the
# loader still answered "open netbsd/kernel: No such file or directory" for every name it tried. The
# second run, with / enlarged by hand, published a 0 MiB disk.
#
# SO THE DEFAULT LAYOUT IS KEPT. What the disk actually lacks is not room, and this walk no longer
# guesses at a number: the repair happens in the shell after the sets land, where it can be seen.
step "the default partition sizes"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 18

step "accept the partition table"
  do
    item
      input text "x"
    item
      input ret
      settle 20

# `No` AGAIN BY DEFAULT.
step "continue - yes, the last chance to quit"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 22

# `x: CONTINUE` AND NOT A RETURN: THIS PAGE IS A SELECTION AND ITS FIRST ENTRY IS NOT A SUBMIT.
#
# MEASURED on the disk an earlier version of this walk published (GPT path): the MBR signature
# `55 aa` was at offset 510 and the boot code at 440 was SIXTEEN ZERO BYTES - `installboot` never ran,
# the firmware loaded nothing, and the machine fell through to the CD-ROM with
#
#	Boot failed: Could not read from CDROM (code 0003)
#
# This page is why. `a: Use BIOS console` is already selected; RETURN re-selects it and stays here.
# `x: Continue` is the entry that commits the choice and runs installboot.
step "install the bios console bootblocks"
  do
    item
      input text "x"
    item
      input ret
      settle 25

step "the full distribution"
  input ret
  settle 18

step "install from the cd-rom"
  input ret
  settle 22

step "the note about the optional sets this medium does not carry"
  input ret
  settle 30

# THE EXTRACTION IS THE LONG WAIT AND IT IS THE ONE THING A FIXED DELAY CANNOT COVER. MEASURED on this
# medium: about four minutes to unpack the 19 selected sets. `settle 400` is the ceiling; the next
# keypress is harmless if it arrives after the page appears, because the page after it is the password
# prompt and a stray return there simply re-asks.
step "wait for the sets to be extracted"
  settle 400

step "the extraction is complete"
  input ret
  settle 14

step "the root password - first entry"
  do
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      settle 5
    item
      input ret
      settle 5

step "the root password - the repeat"
  do
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      settle 5
    item
      input ret
      settle 14

# THE CONFIGURATION MENU. `g` enables sshd and `k` enables xdm; both default to NO and both are what
# the later stages need. Each returns to the same menu, so the two keys are sent separately with the
# redraw between them.
# NOTHING IS PADDED HERE, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE REVERSAL.
#
# An earlier version of this file wrote 1.1 GiB of /dev/urandom into the swap slice purely to push the
# compressed artifact over `stage.yml`'s 512 MiB floor. MEASURED on run 45906011040, that is exactly
# what the published image was:
#
#	disk.qcow2:  2750 MiB        (the real install)
#	bsd-netbsd-install.qcow2:  1611 MiB   (1,100 MiB of it random bytes)
#
# The floor was met with data that is not part of any system, so the size said nothing about the
# contents. It is gone. What belongs in that space is the desktop, which `ssh.walk` installs from
# pkgin; that is real content, it is what the other distributions ship in their install image, and it
# clears the same floor for a reason a reader can check.
#
# `p: Run /bin/sh` on the configuration menu stays: it is where the desktop install reads its own
# state back, and it is the installer's own shell rather than a guess at one.

step "enable sshd"
  do
    item
      input text "g"
    item
      input ret
      settle 14

step "enable xdm, the graphical login"
  do
    item
      input text "k"
    item
      input ret
      settle 14

# AND THE TIMEZONE, WHICH IS `b` ON THAT MENU. Its own menu's first entry is UTC.
step "the timezone menu"
  do
    item
      input text "b"
    item
      input ret
      settle 14

# `x` QUITS THE TIMEZONE LIST, AND A RETURN ALONE DOES NOT.
#
# MEASURED on the run that stalled here: the pane read
#
#	Please choose the timezone that fits you best from the list below
#	Press RETURN to select an entry.
#	Press 'x' followed by RETURN to quit the timezone selection.
#	Default: UTC      Selected: UTC
#
# so the entry was already chosen and the LIST was waiting to be left. A RETURN picks an entry; leaving
# the list is `x` and then RETURN, which is what the pane's own help says.
step "utc, the first entry"
  input ret
  settle 12

step "leave the timezone list"
  do
    item
      input text "x"
    item
      input ret
      settle 16

# `Configure network` IS NOT TOUCHED: MEASURED, it terminates sysinst at its netmask prompt. See the
# header. Everything the disk needs is already written at this point.
step "finish configuring"
  do
    item
      input text "x"
    item
      input ret
      settle 20

# THE INSTALLER MUST UNMOUNT THE DISK, AND KILLING qemu IS WHAT CORRUPTED IT.
#
# MEASURED on the 1.75 GiB disk this walk published, mounted from the installer's own shell:
#
#	# ls -la /mnt | head
#	total 326
#	drwx-----T 2550 root wheel 182272 Nov 26 08:22 lost+found
#	-r--r--r--   1 root wheel  86092 Nov 26 07:45 boot
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel   2048 Nov 26 07:55 etc
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel  46592 Nov 26 07:46 dev
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel    512 Nov 26 07:44 kern
#	# ls /mnt/netbsd
#	ls: /mnt/netbsd: No such file or directory
#
# AND THE REBOOT WAS TRIED TOO, WITH THE SAME RESULT - SO IT IS NOT ONLY THE KILL.
#
# MEASURED on the disk from the run that answered `r` (reboot) at the installer's ending: after the
# machine restarted, the disk still mounted with
#
#	drwx-----T 2550 root wheel 182272  lost+found
#	-r--r--r--   1 root wheel  86092  boot
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel   2048  etc
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel   512  kern
#	# ls /mnt/netbsd          -> No such file or directory
#
# and `/mnt` holds NOTHING BUT `boot cdrom dev etc kern lost+found proc var`. There is no `bin`, no
# `sbin`, no `lib`, no `usr` - the directories `base.tar.xz` is made of. So the sets' files reached the
# disk as 2,550 ORPHANED INODES and their directory tree was never created: the extractor wrote the
# files into a root that had no `bin/` for them to live in.
#

# THE DEVICE NODES EXIST AND THE DISK BEHIND THEM DOES NOT, WHICH NAMES THE DEFECT EXACTLY.
#
# MEASURED in the installer's own shell on that disk:
#
#	# ls /dev/sd* | head
#	/dev/sd0  /dev/sd0a  /dev/sd0b  /dev/sd0c  /dev/sd0d  /dev/sd0e
#	# dkctl sd0 listwedges
#	dkctl: sd0: Device not configured
#	# mount /dev/sd0a /mnt
#	mount: cannot open '/dev/sd0a': Device not configured
#
# So devfs published the names and DKWEDGE never attached a disk behind them - sysinst wrote 1.7 GiB
# through the raw device and the slice it names afterwards does not exist. The same holds for `ld0`
# (`dkctl ld0 listwedges` -> "no wedges configured"). Both disk types, both releases, one defect.
#
# AND A SCSI DISK FAILS THE SAME WAY, SO THE DISK TYPE IS NOT THE VARIABLE EITHER.
#
# MEASURED with the disk attached as `scsi-hd` on `virtio-scsi-pci` - which sysinst lists as
# `sd0 (32G, QEMU HARDDISK)`, its most ordinary path - the install ran to completion (1,743,650,816
# bytes written) and then:
#
#	# fsck -y /dev/rsd0a ; mount /dev/sd0a /mnt
#	mount: cannot open '/dev/sd0a': Device not configured
#	# ls -la /mnt | head
#	total 6
#	drwxr-xr-x 2 root wheel 2048 Sep 15 17:50 .
#	drwxr-xr-x 20 root wheel 4096 Jan  1 1970 ..
#	ls /mnt/netbsd: No such file or directory
#
# `/mnt` holds TWO entries, the mount did not happen at all, and `/netbsd` is not there. So the same
# defect appears on sd, ld and every medium tried: sysinst writes the sets' bytes and produces no
# usable tree, and the mount of the root slice is refused either way.
#
# AND NETBSD 10.2 FAILS THE SAME WAY, SO THIS IS NOT AN 11.1 BUG. MEASURED with the identical walk and
# only the medium swapped (NetBSD-10.2-amd64.iso, sha512 852a43c4... verified):
#
#	ls -la /mnt
#	drwx-----T 2531 root wheel 182272  lost+found
#	-r--r--r--   1 root wheel  86284  boot
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel   2048  etc
#	drwxr-xr-x   2 root wheel    512  kern
#	ls /mnt/netbsd -> No such file or directory
#
# 2,531 lost+found entries, the same eight directories, no /netbsd. So the extractor behaves the same
# on both releases and the version is not the variable. Nothing in this walk can change it: every pane
# was answered as measured, the sets' bytes reach the disk, and the tree they need is never made.
#
# SO THE ENDING IS THE INSTALLER'S OWN REBOOT, WHICH UNMOUNTS. `r` is the third character of
#   Exit to (S)hell, (H)alt or (R)eboot? [reboot]
# and the default is already `reboot`, so the RETURN is what takes it; the `r` before it covers the
# case where the menu highlights something else.
# `h` AND NOT `r`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ENDING.
#
# The installer's last question is
#
#	Exit to (S)hell, (H)alt or (R)eboot? [reboot]
#
# and `r` is the default - which sent every earlier run back into the INSTALLER (the medium is still
# bootindex=0), so the machine never stopped, the stage killed qemu mid-write, and the next fsck found
# a dirty filesystem whose names it threw into lost+found. MEASURED on the disks from those runs:
#
#	drwx-----T 2550 root wheel 182272  lost+found
#	# ls /mnt/netbsd      -> No such file or directory
#
# `h` halts the guest: sysinst syncs, unmounts and powers the machine off, which is what
# `wait_until stopped` below can actually wait for.
# THE ENDING IS TWO PANES: THE COMPLETION NOTICE, THEN `d: Reboot the computer` ON THE MAIN MENU.
#
# THIS IS THE WHOLE FAILURE, AND IT WAS MEASURED. Every earlier run answered the installer's
# `Exit to (S)hell, (H)alt or (R)eboot?` with `r`, which reboots into the INSTALLER - the medium is
# still bootindex=0 - so the machine never stopped, the stage killed qemu mid-write, and the next
# fsck found a dirty filesystem and threw every name into lost+found:
#
#	drwx-----T 2550 root wheel 182272  lost+found
#	# ls /mnt/netbsd      -> No such file or directory
#
# What `x: Finished configuring` actually leads to, MEASURED on the run that finished:
#
#	The installation of NetBSD-11.1 is now complete. The system should boot from hard disk.
#	Hit enter to continue          -> RETURN
#	NetBSD-11.1 Install System
#	  a: Install NetBSD to hard disk
#	  ...
#	  d: Reboot the computer       -> `d` + RETURN
#
# and with that disk, mounted from the installer's shell afterwards:
#
#	# ls /mnt
#	bin  boot  dev  etc  kern  lib  libdata  libexec  mnt  netbsd  proc  rescue  root
#	sbin  stand  tmp  usr  var
#
# 23 entries, `netbsd` among them, and no lost+found shambles. So `d` on the main menu is the ending
# that syncs and unmounts, and `r` at the exit prompt is the one that corrupts.
step "the completion notice"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

# THE CONSOLE IS PUT ON THE SERIAL LINE, BECAUSE THIS GUEST WRITES NOTHING TO IT BY DEFAULT AND THE NEXT
# STAGE HAS NO OTHER CHANNEL IT CAN READ.
#
# MEASURED twice: `wc -c serial.log` is 0 after a full BIOS boot of the published disk. The screen is
# the only console, and once `xdm` stops it draws nothing at all - `see: 2 word(s): a —` - so every
# command the ssh stage types has no readable answer.
#
# `consdev=com0,115200` IN `/boot.cfg` IS THE DOCUMENTED SWITCH, from boot.cfg(5):
#
#	consdev  Changes the console device to that specified in the value.
#
# and `p: Run /bin/sh` on the installer's configuration menu is where the new root is still mounted, so
# the file can be written and read back before the machine stops.
step "open the installer's shell, where /mnt is still mounted"
  do
    item
      input text "p"
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "write the serial console into boot.cfg and prove it"
  do
    item
      input text "echo consdev=com0,115200 > /mnt/boot.cfg; cat /mnt/boot.cfg"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "leave the shell so the installer can finish"
  do
    item
      input text "exit"
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

# `d: Reboot the computer` SYNCS AND UNMOUNTS; THEN THE MACHINE BOOTS THE MEDIUM AGAIN.
#
# MEASURED on CI run 45890377885, the first run with this ending: the disk came out 1,643 MiB with
# every set in it, and the only failure was the `wait_until stopped` after it -
#
#	status: failed step 31/32 "wait for the machine to stop on its own"
#
# because `d` REBOOTS: sysinst syncs and unmounts, then the firmware starts the medium again (it is
# still bootindex=0) and the guest is alive on the installer's language menu. So the machine never
# stops, and waiting for it to stop is waiting for something that cannot happen.
#
# WHAT MATTERS IS THE UNMOUNT, AND IT HAS ALREADY HAPPENED BY THE TIME THE LANGUAGE MENU IS BACK: that
# is the same disk whose `/` listed
#
#	bin boot dev etc kern lib libdata libexec mnt netbsd proc rescue root sbin stand tmp usr var
#
# when this walk's ending was driven by hand in the pod. So this step waits for the language menu -
# proof the reboot completed and the disk was released - and the stage then stops the machine.
step "the reboot reached the medium's own menu, which means the disk was released"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 15
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name back
    until
      bind released
      source back
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 1920, 1080]
      rect_at [1920, 1080]
      scale 2
      # THE REBOOT LANDS ON THE INSTALL SYSTEM'S MAIN MENU, NOT ON THE LANGUAGE LIST. MEASURED: it
      # re-enters the medium at `NetBSD-11.1 Install System / a: Install NetBSD to hard disk`. Either
      # menu proves the reboot ran and the disk was released, so both are accepted.
      words [[install, netbsd, to, hard, disk], [installation, messages, in, english], [welcome, to, the, netbsd]]
    max 40

step "stop the machine"
  stop "{{ shutdown }}"
  timeout 300

step "publish the disk"
    save "{{ out }}"
